"Done. Created `deadlock_sim.py` (96 lines) in the workspace, syntax-checked. How it works:\n\n```mermaid\nsequenceDiagram\n    participant A as Thread A\n    participant DB as SQLite (migration.db)\n    participant B as Thread B\n    A->>DB: BEGIN (deferred) + read (SHARED lock)\n    B->>DB: BEGIN (deferred) + read (SHARED lock)\n    A->>DB: UPDATE account 1 (RESERVED)\n    B->>DB: UPDATE account 2 (RESERVED)\n    Note over A,B: sleep 0.1s, race window open\n    A->>DB: UPDATE account 2 -> blocked (B holds RESERVED)\n    B->>DB: UPDATE account 1 -> blocked (A holds RESERVED)\n    Note over A,B: crisscross: SQLITE_BUSY for one side\n```\n\nKey design points:\n\n- **Two separate connections**, one per thread (`sqlite3` connections aren't shared across threads)\n- **Explicit `BEGIN` (deferred)** with `isolation_level=None` so each thread holds a transaction across both updates\n- **Opposite orders**: A does account 1 then 2; B does account 2 then 1\n- **0.1s sleep** between updates widens the window where each thread holds a write lock the other needs, making `database is locked` (`SQLITE_BUSY`) very likely\n- **Start barrier** releases both threads at the same instant for reproducible contention\n- Failure paths **rollback** cleanly so the database is left consistent, and a busy timeout of 5s applies before giving up\n\nI only ran a syntax check, not the script, so the balances are untouched. Run it with `python3 deadlock_sim.py` when you want to see the contention in action."